Here's the full pipeline, phase by phase.

---

## Phase 1: Registration — `app.use(...)`

`src/hono-base.ts:157–168`

```ts
this.use = (arg1, ...handlers) => {
  if (typeof arg1 === 'string') {
    this.#path = arg1
  } else {
    this.#path = '*'        // no path given → wildcard
    handlers.unshift(arg1)
  }
  handlers.forEach((handler) => {
    this.#addRoute(METHOD_NAME_ALL, this.#path, handler)
  })
}
```

`app.use(mw)` normalizes to path `'*'` and method `ALL`, then delegates to `#addRoute`.

`src/hono-base.ts:385–391`

```ts
#addRoute(method, path, handler) {
  path = mergePath(this._basePath, path)
  this.router.add(method, path, [handler, routeRecord])
  this.routes.push(routeRecord)
}
```

The handler is added to the underlying router (Trie/RegExp/Linear — whichever was constructed) as the value `[handler, routeRecord]`. **Nothing is executed yet; this is pure storage.**

Route handlers registered with `app.get(path, h)` go through the same `#addRoute`, just with `method='GET'` and a concrete path. Both middleware and handlers live in the same router, ordered by registration time.

---

## Phase 2: Dispatch — a request arrives

`src/hono-base.ts:400–460` (`#dispatch`)

```ts
const path = this.getPath(request, { env })
const matchResult = this.router.match(method, path)
// matchResult[0] is [[handler, routeRecord], params][]
```

The router returns every rule whose method+pattern matched, in registration order — so a `use('*')` registered before a `get('/foo')` appears earlier in the array.

A fresh `Context` is created (`src/hono-base.ts:415–421`), then:

```ts
// Fast path: skip compose for a single handler
if (matchResult[0].length === 1) {          // :424
  res = matchResult[0][0][0][0](c, next)    // :427
}

// Slow path: multiple handlers → compose
const composed = compose(matchResult[0], this.errorHandler, this.#notFoundHandler)  // :444
await composed(c)
```

---

## Phase 3: `compose` — building the chain

`src/compose.ts:15–73`

`compose` takes the flat `middleware` array and returns a closure. When called with `(context, outerNext?)` it immediately calls:

```ts
return dispatch(0)
```

`dispatch(i)` is the recursive engine:

```ts
async function dispatch(i: number): Promise<Context> {
  if (i <= index) throw new Error('next() called multiple times')  // :33–35
  index = i

  let handler
  if (middleware[i]) {
    handler = middleware[i][0][0]          // unwrap [[fn, route], params]
    context.req.routeIndex = i             // :43–44, used by c.req.routeIndex
  } else {
    handler = (i === middleware.length && next) || undefined  // :46, outerNext
  }

  if (handler) {
    try {
      res = await handler(context, () => dispatch(i + 1))   // :51  ← THE CHAIN
    } catch (err) {
      if (err instanceof Error && onError) {
        res = await onError(err, context)   // :55
      } else { throw err }
    }
  } else {
    if (!context.finalized && onNotFound) {
      res = await onNotFound(context)       // :63
    }
  }

  if (res && (!context.finalized || isError)) context.res = res
  return context
}
```

The critical line is `:51`: each handler receives `() => dispatch(i + 1)` as its `next` argument. **Calling `await next()` inside a middleware is literally calling `dispatch(i + 1)`**, which runs the next entry in the matched array.

---

## Execution order for `await next()`

Given:
```ts
app.use(async (c, next) => { /* A-before */; await next(); /* A-after */ })
app.use(async (c, next) => { /* B-before */; await next(); /* B-after */ })
app.get('/foo', (c) => c.text('handler'))
```

All three are in `matchResult[0]` in that order. Execution:

```
dispatch(0) → runs A-before → calls next() → dispatch(1)
  dispatch(1) → runs B-before → calls next() → dispatch(2)
    dispatch(2) → runs handler → returns Response
  B-after resumes
A-after resumes
```

This is the standard onion model. The `index` guard at `compose.ts:33–35` ensures `next()` can only advance, never re-enter the same frame.

---

## After the chain

Back in `#dispatch` (`hono-base.ts:446–459`):

```ts
const context = await composed(c)
if (!context.finalized) {
  throw new Error('Context is not finalized. Did you forget to return a Response…?')
}
return context.res
```

`context.finalized` is set inside `Context` when `c.res` is assigned a real `Response`. If no handler wrote a response and `onNotFound` was also skipped (e.g. a middleware forgot `await next()`), Hono throws here rather than returning garbage.

---

**Summary of the data path:**

| What | Where |
|---|---|
| `app.use(mw)` stores handler | `hono-base.ts:157–168` → `#addRoute` → `router.add(...)` |
| Router match on request | `hono-base.ts:413` |
| Single-handler fast path | `hono-base.ts:424–441` |
| Multi-handler compose call | `hono-base.ts:444` |
| Chain execution loop | `compose.ts:32–71` (`dispatch`) |
| `next()` = advance to next handler | `compose.ts:51` `() => dispatch(i + 1)` |
| Error routing | `compose.ts:53–58` → `onError` |
| 404 fallthrough | `compose.ts:62–64` → `onNotFound` |
